<div class="mw-parser-output"><h1>Set (230)</h1>
<p>Usage:
</p>
<pre> &gt; set &lt;unit&gt; &lt;field&gt; &lt;arguments&gt;
</pre>
<p>This is the command you use, if you want to fiddle with individual fields in a
unit. As there are a LOT of fields to set, i will not list them here. The
command will list them, if you just give a unit name. However it is essential,
that you understand the argument types.
</p><p>The unit can be room (the room you are in), or anything you can name: in 
your inventory or equipment, here, or anywhere in the world. Fields can be 
shortened, as long as the start is clear. Some fields need a higher level 
than 230; changing another player, or anything they carry or wear, needs 
level 245 at least. Money can only be changed at level 255. The command is 
logged.
</p>
<pre> &lt;value&gt;&#160;: a value. beware not to enter a number out of range.
 &lt;type&gt;&#160;: a type. to list possible types give it an 'X' as type
 &lt;string&gt;&#160;: a single string.
    (enter description): this gives you the multi-line editor.
    End with a '@' on a single line.
 &lt;unitpath&gt;: a 'path' to the unit, specifying its zonename and itemname.
 &lt;keyword&gt;&#160;: a single keyword.
 &lt;direction&gt;&#160;: direction can be any of the six: n s e w u d
 &lt;bitlist&gt;&#160;: a list of bitnames. nothing indicates no set bits.
             separate bitnames with a single '|'.
</pre>
<p>For add-name and xxxdirection info:
</p>
<pre>  &lt;names&gt; is a comma separated list of values e.g. city guard, guard
</pre>
<p>For extra data (add-extra, add-info, add-quest):
</p>
<pre>  &lt;names&gt;;&lt;ints&gt; first a comma separated list of names, optionally a semicolon 
                 followed by a comma separated list of integers.
</pre>
<p>Examples:
</p>
<pre> &gt; set rabbit add-name killer rabbit, carrot muncher
 &gt; set papi add-info $professions,barbarian,dark knight,necromancer;1,2,3
 &gt; set john del-extra ,     // This example removes the an empty name, i.e. the default description
</pre>
<p><br />
Combinations of the above occur.
</p><p>See Also:
</p>
<pre>&gt; <a cmd='#'>Wizhelp Setskill</a>
&gt; <a cmd='#'>Wizhelp Wstat</a>
&gt; <a cmd='#'>Wizhelp Nset</a>
</pre>




</div>